// Bug 1: getFieldValues() violates equals() reflexivity
import org.jabref.model.entry.BibEntry;
import org.jabref.model.entry.types.StandardEntryType;
import java.util.Collection;

public class JabRefBug1 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BibEntry entry = new BibEntry(StandardEntryType.Article);

        Collection<String> values = entry.getFieldValues();

        // This should ALWAYS print true in Java, but it evaluates to false
        System.out.println("Is collection equal to itself? " + values.equals(values));
    }
}
```
2. Compile it using below command:
`javac -cp "jablib\build\classes\java\main;jablib\build\dependencies\*" JabRefBug1.java`
3. Run it using below command:
`C:\git-repositories\jabref>java -cp ".;jablib\build\classes\java\main;jablib\build\dependencies\*" JabRefBug1`

Current output:
Is collection equal to itself? false

### Appendix

Found this bug using automated contract testing with Randoop and tested using above Minimal Reproducible Example (MRE). When we call bibEntry.getFieldValues(), it returns a Collection<String>. However, this collection violates the fundamental Java Object.equals() contract, which states an object must be equal to itself. Because it returns false, placing these collections into HashSets or HashMaps could cause silent data corruption or memory leaks in JabRef.

